# Rank 1298 / CVE-2015-8789 (Cisco TALOS-CAN-0037): libebml's
# EbmlMaster::Read() (src/EbmlMaster.cpp) reads a run of sibling child
# elements in a loop. FindNextElement/the recursive ElementLevelA->Read()
# call communicate "the element I actually found belongs to an ancestor
# level, N levels up" back to the caller through two out-parameters,
# UpperEltFound (a countdown) and FoundElt (the element itself); the normal
# path (after the finite/infinite-size handling) decrements UpperEltFound
# and, once it reaches exactly this level (0), does `ElementLevelA =
# FoundElt; continue;` so the loop absorbs it as this level's own element.
#
# The infinite-size branch skipped all of that: `if (DeleteElement) delete
# ElementLevelA; break;` returns straight out of the loop (and the function)
# without ever inspecting UpperEltFound or handing FoundElt back up. If the
# nested ElementLevelA->Read() call that just ran found such an ancestor
# element while ElementLevelA itself has infinite size, the signal -- and
# FoundElt -- is silently dropped at this level. The caller one recursion
# level up still receives the stale, undecremented UpperEltFound and later
# rediscovers/re-inserts the very same FoundElt a second time, so it ends
# up a member of two different EbmlMaster instances' ElementList at once --
# freed once when each owner's element tree is torn down (double free), and
# used after the first free in between (CWE-415/CWE-416).
#
# Fix 88409e2a94dd3b40ff81d08bf6d92f486d036b24 adds eight lines and deletes
# nothing: the infinite-size branch now checks UpperEltFound before
# breaking, exactly mirroring the finite-size path -- decrement it, goto
# processCrc if it still belongs further up, else take over FoundElt as
# ElementLevelA so the enclosing loop absorbs it here instead of leaking
# the signal to the caller. A guard-only fix leaves every call site and
# every identifier in the function byte-identical across the revision
# pair, so no binding anchored on a call or a name can separate the trees
# -- unlike the krb5 rank of this same gap family, whose fix deleted the
# helper outright and so fell to an exact-name binding.
#
# Rechecked (three `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB`
# runs per revision over the real checkout, vulnerable parent 24e5cd7 and
# fix 88409e2a94): 473 findings on every run, both sides, identical rule
# histogram (62 VYQL-MEM-006, 12 VYQL-MEM-016, 6 VYQL-MEM-002) and zero
# VYQL-MEM-007/VYQL-MEM-008 anywhere; the only line-level difference in
# the whole finding set is the three VYQL-MEM-006 code.NullableDeref rows
# inside Read() shifting +8 where the guard is inserted. The current cpp
# corpus does label this function (the first pass saw nothing at all):
# `vyql match` reports code.IndexAccessSink, code.IntegerSizeArithmetic
# (both sides of the Sink pair), code.PointerUse and code.NullableDeref
# from cpp.flags -- but none of those labels touches the discriminating
# construct and none feeds a rule that reports here. `vyql resolve` on an
# extract still leaves ElementLevelA->Read, ElementList.push_back and the
# FindNextElement path unresolved; the full-tree scan resolves what it can
# and the finding set still does not move.
#
# Probe-measured token parity (one probe binding at a time, `scan --cache
# off` on both revisions of the extracted function): every AST-derived
# context token the added guard produces already exists in the finite-size
# path of BOTH revisions -- identifier:UpperEltFound,
# binary:UpperEltFound>0, binary:UpperEltFound>0||MaxSizeToRead<=0,
# assign:ElementLevelA=FoundElt and the recursive Read call's call_arg:
# entries each fire on both sides (1=1), because ccStructuredContextTokens
# dedups, so the fix adds zero new structured tokens. The single
# discriminating route is the raw body-text token (`node.token contains
# "--UpperEltFound"`: 0 on the parent, 1 on the fix) -- the fixing
# commit's own pre-decrement spelling, which a genuinely-enforcing variant
# written `UpperEltFound--`, `UpperEltFound -= 1` or any post-decrement
# guard form would not contain; keying the vulnerable side on the absence
# of that exact spelling is the fix's layout, not a library-documented
# fact, and the body-text route is whole-function and comment-silenceable.
# (`if (UpperEltFound)` does not match at all -- the body token is
# whitespace-stripped.) Exhaustive grep over both trees: the pre-decrement
# spelling occurs exactly once, in the fixed revision's added guard; and
# both revisions still contain a second `delete ElementLevelA;`
# immediately followed by `break;` with no UpperEltFound consult (the
# dummy-element branch, untouched by the fix and safe only because no
# recursive Read precedes it on that path), so even a path-sensitive
# delete-then-break fact would fire on the fixed tree unless it also knew
# that only the recursive call writes the signal.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap, held on recheck. VYQL-MEM-008
# (PotentialDoubleFree) needs code.PointerFree reaches code.PointerFree,
# and pointerFree91 keys `node.path ~= "free"`; a C++ `delete` lowers to
# no free-named node, and even a delete-aware PointerFree binding would
# fire identically on both trees because the fix adds and removes no
# delete. What separates the trees is whether the pending out-parameter
# signal is consulted on the path between the recursive call and the early
# exit -- a path-sensitive, interprocedural ownership fact (only the
# callee writes UpperEltFound through its reference parameter), the same
# out-parameter/ownership-transfer family recorded unmechanical at ranks
# 1155, 1157, 1281, 1283 and 1285. Not mechanical, re-affirmed: closing it
# needs a new dataflow relation, not a correction to an existing
# extraction, and the weaker text-level forms are either the fix's own
# spelling (measured above) or broad enough to flag every early exit after
# an ownership transfer.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, rechecked; both revision
# blocks pinned rejecting VYQL-MEM-007/VYQL-MEM-008, which no rule reports
# at either revision.

test "rank1298: infinite-size child drops a pending upper-level element instead of absorbing it"
  lang cpp
  file EbmlMaster.cpp
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-008
  code
  ```
  void EbmlMaster::Read(EbmlStream & inDataStream, const EbmlSemanticContext & sContext, int & UpperEltFound, EbmlElement * & FoundElt, bool AllowDummyElt, ScopeMode ReadFully)
  {
    EbmlElement * ElementLevelA;
    ElementList.clear();
    uint64 MaxSizeToRead = IsFiniteSize() ? GetSize() : 0x7FFFFFFF;

    ElementLevelA = inDataStream.FindNextElement(sContext, UpperEltFound, MaxSizeToRead, AllowDummyElt);
    while (ElementLevelA != NULL && UpperEltFound <= 0 && MaxSizeToRead > 0) {
      ElementLevelA->Read(inDataStream, EBML_CONTEXT(ElementLevelA), UpperEltFound, FoundElt, AllowDummyElt, ReadFully);

      bool DeleteElement = true;
      if (ElementLevelA->ValueIsSet() || (ReadFully != SCOPE_ALL_DATA)) {
        ElementList.push_back(ElementLevelA);
        DeleteElement = false;
      }

      if (ElementLevelA->IsFiniteSize()) {
        ElementLevelA->SkipData(inDataStream, EBML_CONTEXT(ElementLevelA));
        if (DeleteElement)
          delete ElementLevelA;
      } else {
        if (DeleteElement)
          delete ElementLevelA;
        break;
      }

      if (UpperEltFound > 0) {
        UpperEltFound--;
        if (UpperEltFound > 0 || MaxSizeToRead <= 0)
          goto processCrc;
        ElementLevelA = FoundElt;
        continue;
      }

      ElementLevelA = inDataStream.FindNextElement(sContext, UpperEltFound, MaxSizeToRead, AllowDummyElt);
    }

  processCrc:
    return;
  }
  ```

test "rank1298: infinite-size child hands a pending upper-level element back before breaking"
  lang cpp
  file EbmlMaster.cpp
  reject VYQL-MEM-007
  reject VYQL-MEM-008
  code
  ```
  void EbmlMaster::Read(EbmlStream & inDataStream, const EbmlSemanticContext & sContext, int & UpperEltFound, EbmlElement * & FoundElt, bool AllowDummyElt, ScopeMode ReadFully)
  {
    EbmlElement * ElementLevelA;
    ElementList.clear();
    uint64 MaxSizeToRead = IsFiniteSize() ? GetSize() : 0x7FFFFFFF;

    ElementLevelA = inDataStream.FindNextElement(sContext, UpperEltFound, MaxSizeToRead, AllowDummyElt);
    while (ElementLevelA != NULL && UpperEltFound <= 0 && MaxSizeToRead > 0) {
      ElementLevelA->Read(inDataStream, EBML_CONTEXT(ElementLevelA), UpperEltFound, FoundElt, AllowDummyElt, ReadFully);

      bool DeleteElement = true;
      if (ElementLevelA->ValueIsSet() || (ReadFully != SCOPE_ALL_DATA)) {
        ElementList.push_back(ElementLevelA);
        DeleteElement = false;
      }

      if (ElementLevelA->IsFiniteSize()) {
        ElementLevelA->SkipData(inDataStream, EBML_CONTEXT(ElementLevelA));
        if (DeleteElement)
          delete ElementLevelA;
      } else {
        if (DeleteElement)
          delete ElementLevelA;

        if (UpperEltFound) {
          --UpperEltFound;
          if (UpperEltFound > 0 || MaxSizeToRead <= 0)
            goto processCrc;
          ElementLevelA = FoundElt;
        }

        break;
      }

      if (UpperEltFound > 0) {
        UpperEltFound--;
        if (UpperEltFound > 0 || MaxSizeToRead <= 0)
          goto processCrc;
        ElementLevelA = FoundElt;
        continue;
      }

      ElementLevelA = inDataStream.FindNextElement(sContext, UpperEltFound, MaxSizeToRead, AllowDummyElt);
    }

  processCrc:
    return;
  }
  ```
